1
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:40,530
No one wants to be in an accident, that's why we take so many precautions.

2
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:44,970
We wear personal protective equipment and carefully select each piece of apparatus that

3
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:47,010
we use.

4
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:51,530
But accidents do happen, no matter how many precautions we take.

5
00:00:51,530 --> 00:01:09,270
When they do, quick action is imperative, and a safety shower or eyewash can be a godsend.

6
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,610
There are several types of safety showers and eyewash stations.

7
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:15,250
The best are simple.

8
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:18,730
With one movement, the water starts the flow.

9
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:23,710
The stream continues until a second movement is made to turn it off.

10
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:29,170
The flow of water in a safety shower must be strong enough to immediately drench the victim.

11
00:01:29,170 --> 00:01:36,050
The unit should also provide enough water for at least 15 minutes of continuous use.

12
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:39,430
Eyewashes need to produce a soft stream or spray of aerated water.

13
00:01:39,430 --> 00:01:44,800
This should also last for at least 15 minutes.

14
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:49,800
Acids and eyewashes should be located wherever corrosive, infectious, or other hazardous substances

15
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:53,650
exist.

16
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:55,530
Corrosives can be especially harmful.

17
00:01:55,530 --> 00:02:00,810
They can often cause severe damage to the skin and eyes.

18
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:07,970
Corrosives include strong acids and bases and both dehydrating and oxidizing agents.

19
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:13,290
Nitric acid, for instance, produces a slow-healing, painful burn.

20
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:18,530
And hydroxide, a base can inflict severe damage to the eyes.

21
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:22,170
Of course, when you're working with these types of substances, you should always wear

22
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:26,030
personal protective equipment.

23
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:29,450
You should also read the substance's safety data sheets.

24
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:34,890
These will tell you each substance's ingredients, properties, and hazards.

25
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:39,170
Your facility's chemical hygiene plan will often provide additional information on the

26
00:02:39,170 --> 00:02:51,940
safe use of the chemicals.

27
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:56,380
No matter how safe you think you are, you should know the locations of the safety showers

28
00:02:56,380 --> 00:03:01,020
and eyewashes in your area and how to use them.

29
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:06,340
In fact, you should know how to find them with your eyes closed.

30
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:10,860
It's important to make it easy for any victim to reach the showers and eyewashes in your

31
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:11,860
labs.

32
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,900
Keep access routes free of equipment and supplies.

33
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,520
Shower areas themselves must also remain clear.

34
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,680
The equipment should be routinely tested to make sure it's kept in peak condition.

35
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:30,400
Accreditation agencies such as CAP and JCAHO require that showers and eyewashes be stress-tested

36
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:31,760
periodically.

37
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:37,120
Your supervisor or safety manager will put together a schedule.

38
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,900
Showers and eyewashes must function properly at all times.

39
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,440
The water should be checked to make sure it's potable, not stagnant.

40
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:59,890
Results of the testing should be marked on a tag, dated, and initialed.

41
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:03,170
If you're splashed by a hazardous substance, don't panic.

42
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:09,370
Call out for help and get to a shower or eyewash, depending on the extent of the splash.

43
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:12,610
If you find yourself helping with a victim, take charge.

44
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:14,250
Make sure they are completely drenched.

45
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:18,050
It usually requires two helpers to handle everything.

46
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:22,630
Clothing should be soaked all the way through.

47
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:24,910
Remove all personal protective equipment.

48
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:28,830
Take special care with safety glasses and goggles.

49
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:32,270
The victim's clothing should be removed to at least the underwear.

50
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:34,030
This is no time for modesty.

51
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:37,270
Don't forget to remove their shoes as well.

52
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:41,430
If there isn't a retention basin under the shower, the wastewater should be surrounded

53
00:04:41,430 --> 00:04:43,190
with absorbent material.

54
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:48,400
This will help to prevent the spread of contamination.

55
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:52,600
After the initial deluge, the victim can move to a restroom or locker room to continue

56
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:53,720
the shower.

57
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:57,240
The victim should then remove the rest of their clothing.

58
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:03,390
The entire showering time should be no less than 15 minutes.

59
00:05:03,390 --> 00:05:06,030
Anyone helping the victim may also be at risk.

60
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:10,470
They will inevitably get wet, and while the shower dilutes the chemical that they're exposed

61
00:05:10,470 --> 00:05:16,460
to, they may also need to decontaminate.

62
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,140
Disposal of the shower water can often be an issue.

63
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:23,580
In some situations, the water will need to be disposed of as chemical waste.

64
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:29,340
This is especially true if the spilled substance is radioactive.

65
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:33,140
Dressed off clothing should be decontaminated prior to normal laundering.

66
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:39,350
If the spilled substance is particularly hazardous, the clothing should be disposed of.

67
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:42,910
If there's a drain under the shower, you must know whether it empties into a storage

68
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:44,630
tank or the sewer.

69
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:50,760
If it's the sewer, your supervisor may need to alert outside agencies.

70
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:55,400
And remember, if a shower does have a drain, water should be kept in the trap at all

71
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:56,400
times.

72
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,560
This will keep sewer gases from seeping up into the room.

73
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:12,930
These gases can be both flammable and toxic.

74
00:06:12,930 --> 00:06:17,250
If a chemical splashes into your eyes, an eye wash is what you should use.

75
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:22,850
As we've said, it should provide a gentle, continuous stream of aerated water.

76
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:26,050
You must get to the eye wash as quickly as possible.

77
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:30,690
Hold the affected eye open with your fingers to get a complete rinse, both under and behind

78
00:06:30,690 --> 00:06:32,930
the eyelid.

79
00:06:32,930 --> 00:06:37,450
If only one eye was splashed, do not allow the water to transfer the contamination to

80
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:39,930
the other eye.

81
00:06:39,930 --> 00:06:43,410
Once again, wash the area for at least 15 minutes.

82
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:47,530
The eye may feel better before then, but the damaging effects of many chemicals are

83
00:06:47,530 --> 00:06:51,390
sometimes slow acting.

84
00:06:51,390 --> 00:06:55,890
Some portable eye wash units don't supply 15 minutes of water, so they can only be

85
00:06:55,890 --> 00:06:58,050
used for a quick initial wash.

86
00:06:58,050 --> 00:07:03,370
The victim will still need a complete 15-minute rinse.

87
00:07:03,370 --> 00:07:07,230
Most handheld drench hoses require constant hand pressure to operate.

88
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:11,870
This doesn't allow both hands to be free for manipulating the injured eye, so a drench

89
00:07:11,870 --> 00:07:16,350
hose shouldn't be used as an eye wash.

90
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:20,970
Small eye wash bottles should only be used when there is absolutely nothing else.

91
00:07:20,970 --> 00:07:25,070
Then the victim will need to get to another source of water immediately.

92
00:07:25,070 --> 00:07:29,950
Remember, safety showers and eye washes are emergency equipment.

93
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:34,390
After they're used, the victim should get immediate medical attention.

94
00:07:34,390 --> 00:07:43,380
And all uses of showers and eye washes must be reported to your supervisor.

95
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:48,780
Safety showers and eye washes are as fundamental to the laboratory as hoods and pipettes, and

96
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:52,420
you need to know how to use them in case of an emergency.

97
00:07:52,420 --> 00:07:54,820
Let's review.

98
00:07:54,820 --> 00:08:00,780
Know the locations of the safety showers and eye washes in your laboratory.

99
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:05,140
Keep roots to showers and eye washes clear.

100
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:09,860
Know what steps to take if a chemical splash does occur.

101
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:15,180
And get splashed victim's medical attention immediately.

102
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:19,420
By keeping your lab's safety showers and eye washes in good shape and knowing how to

103
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:25,020
use them, you may be able to prevent a serious injury, even save someone's eyesight.
